{
  "id": "ml-ai-195",
  "title": "Pandas DataFrame transpose() Method - Swap Rows and Columns - GeeksforGeeks",
  "content": "<div class=\"html-chunk\"><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Transposing a Pandas DataFrame means switching rows and columns. That means row labels become column headers and column headers become row labels. It is useful when we want to change orientation of our data for better readability and analysis. In this article, we will see some examples to understand it better.</span></p><blockquote><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Syntax</strong></b><span>: DataFrame.transpose(*args, **kwargs)</span></p></blockquote><ul><li value=\"1\"><b><strong>args, kwargs</strong></b><span>: Optional arguments which are not needed for basic use.</span></li></ul><h2 id=\"example-1-basic-transpose\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Example 1: Basic Transpose</span></h2><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Here we are creating a simple DataFrame and transposing it. We will implement it using </span><a href=\"#/article/python/python-778\"><span>Pandas</span></a><span> library. </span></p><gfg-tabs data-run-ide=\"true\" data-mode=\"light\">\n            <gfg-tab slot=\"tab\">Python</gfg-tab>\n<gfg-panel slot=\"panel\" data-code-lang=\"python3\" data-main-code-start=\"None\" data-main-code-end=\"None\">\n    <code class=\"language-python3\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"nn\">pandas</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"nn\">pd</span>\n<span class=\"n\">data</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s1\">'Name'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s1\">'ANSH'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'VANSH'</span><span class=\"p\">],</span> <span class=\"s1\">'Age'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">25</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">30</span><span class=\"p\">]}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">df</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">pd</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">DataFrame</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">data</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Original DataFrame:\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">df</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">transposed_df</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">df</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">transpose</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s2\">Transposed DataFrame:\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">transposed_df</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</pre></div></code>\n</gfg-panel></gfg-tabs><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Output:</strong></b></p><figure class=\"image\"><img src=\"./images/ml-ai-195-img-1.webp\" alt=\"transpose1\" width=\"215\" height=\"201\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150048865513/transpose1.webp 215w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150048865513/transpose1-100.webp 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150048865513/transpose1-200.webp 200w\"><figcaption>Basic Transpose</figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>This example shows how rows become columns and vice versa.</span></p><h2 id=\"example-2-transposing-specific-columns\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Example 2: Transposing Specific Columns</span></h2><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>We can also transpose specific columns by specifying them while transposing.</span></p><gfg-tabs data-run-ide=\"true\" data-mode=\"light\">\n            <gfg-tab slot=\"tab\">Python</gfg-tab>\n<gfg-panel slot=\"panel\" data-code-lang=\"python3\" data-main-code-start=\"None\" data-main-code-end=\"None\">\n    <code class=\"language-python3\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"nn\">pandas</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"nn\">pd</span>\n<span class=\"n\">data</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s1\">'Fee'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">22000</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">25000</span><span class=\"p\">],</span> <span class=\"s1\">'Duration'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">]}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">df</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">pd</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">DataFrame</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">data</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">transposed_fee</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">df</span><span class=\"p\">[[</span><span class=\"s1\">'Fee'</span><span class=\"p\">]]</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">transpose</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">transposed_fee</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</pre></div></code>\n</gfg-panel></gfg-tabs><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Output</strong></b><span>:</span></p><figure class=\"image\"><img src=\"./images/ml-ai-195-img-2.webp\" alt=\"transpose2\" width=\"206\" height=\"75\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150133231305/transpose2.webp 206w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150133231305/transpose2-100.webp 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150133231305/transpose2-200.webp 200w\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Transposing specific column</figcaption></figure><h2 id=\"example-3-transposing-with-mixed-data-types-and-datetime-index\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Example 3: Transposing with Mixed Data Types and DateTime Index</span></h2><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>If the DataFrame contains mixed data types and custom indices transposing works in the same way.</span></p><gfg-tabs data-run-ide=\"true\" data-mode=\"light\">\n            <gfg-tab slot=\"tab\">Python</gfg-tab>\n<gfg-panel slot=\"panel\" data-code-lang=\"python3\" data-main-code-start=\"None\" data-main-code-end=\"None\">\n    <code class=\"language-python3\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"nn\">pandas</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"nn\">pd</span>\n<span class=\"n\">df</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">pd</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">DataFrame</span><span class=\"p\">({</span>\n    <span class=\"s1\">'Weight'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">45</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">88</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">56</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">15</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">71</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"s1\">'Name'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s1\">'DEV'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'ANSHIKA'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'FARHAN'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'SHRIDHAR'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'VAMIKA'</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"s1\">'Age'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">14</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">25</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">55</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">8</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">21</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"p\">},</span> <span class=\"n\">index</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"n\">pd</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">date_range</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s1\">'2010-10-09 08:45'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">periods</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">freq</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s1\">'h'</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Original DataFrame:\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">df</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">transposed_df</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">df</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">transpose</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s2\">Transposed DataFrame:\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">transposed_df</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</pre></div></code>\n</gfg-panel></gfg-tabs><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Output</strong></b><span>:</span></p><figure class=\"image\"><img src=\"./images/ml-ai-195-img-3.webp\" alt=\"trasnpose-3\" width=\"695\" height=\"409\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150237797852/trasnpose-3.webp 695w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150237797852/trasnpose-3-100.webp 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150237797852/trasnpose-3-200.webp 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150237797852/trasnpose-3-300.webp 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150237797852/trasnpose-3-660.webp 660w\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Transposing Mixed Data Types</figcaption></figure><h2 id=\"example-4-handling-missing-values-in-transposition\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Example 4: Handling Missing Values in Transpose</span></h2><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The transpose() function also handles missing values in the same way as other operation.</span></p><gfg-tabs data-run-ide=\"true\" data-mode=\"light\">\n            <gfg-tab slot=\"tab\">Python</gfg-tab>\n<gfg-panel slot=\"panel\" data-code-lang=\"python3\" data-main-code-start=\"None\" data-main-code-end=\"None\">\n    <code class=\"language-python3\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"nn\">pandas</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"nn\">pd</span>\n<span class=\"n\">df</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">pd</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">DataFrame</span><span class=\"p\">({</span><span class=\"s2\">\"A\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">12</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"kc\">None</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">],</span><span class=\"s2\">\"B\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">54</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"kc\">None</span><span class=\"p\">],</span><span class=\"s2\">\"C\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">20</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">16</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">11</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">8</span><span class=\"p\">],</span><span class=\"s2\">\"D\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">14</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"kc\">None</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"p\">]},</span>\n                  <span class=\"n\">index</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s1\">'Row_1'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'Row_2'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'Row_3'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'Row_4'</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s1\">'Row_5'</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Original DataFrame:\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">df</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">transposed_df</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">df</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">transpose</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s2\">Transposed DataFrame:\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">transposed_df</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</pre></div></code>\n</gfg-panel></gfg-tabs><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Output</strong></b><span>:</span></p><figure class=\"image\"><img src=\"./images/ml-ai-195-img-4.webp\" alt=\"transpose-4\" width=\"387\" height=\"336\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150423793998/transpose-4.webp 387w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150423793998/transpose-4-100.webp 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150423793998/transpose-4-200.webp 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250426150423793998/transpose-4-300.webp 300w\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Handling Missing Values</figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Transposing a DataFrame is simple in Pandas library which  allows us to quickly change the way our data is structured and helps in exploring it from a different perspective.</span></p></div>",
  "textContent": "Transposing a Pandas DataFrame means switching rows and columns. That means row labels become column headers and column headers become row labels. It is useful when we want to change orientation of our data for better readability and analysis. In this article, we will see some examples to understand it better.Syntax: DataFrame.transpose(*args, **kwargs)args, kwargs: Optional arguments which are not needed for basic use.Example 1: Basic TransposeHere we are creating a simple DataFrame and transposing it. We will implement it using Pandas library. \n            Python\n\n    import pandas as pd\ndata = {'Name': ['ANSH', 'VANSH'], 'Age': [25, 30]}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\nprint(\"Original DataFrame:\")\nprint(df)\ntransposed_df = df.transpose()\nprint(\"\\nTransposed DataFrame:\")\nprint(transposed_df)\n\nOutput:Basic TransposeThis example shows how rows become columns and vice versa.Example 2: Transposing Specific ColumnsWe can also transpose specific columns by specifying them while transposing.\n            Python\n\n    import pandas as pd\ndata = {'Fee': [22000, 25000], 'Duration': [3, 4]}\ndf = pd.DataFrame(data)\ntransposed_fee = df[['Fee']].transpose()\nprint(transposed_fee)\n\nOutput:Transposing specific columnExample 3: Transposing with Mixed Data Types and DateTime IndexIf the DataFrame contains mixed data types and custom indices transposing works in the same way.\n            Python\n\n    import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Weight': [45, 88, 56, 15, 71],\n    'Name': ['DEV', 'ANSHIKA', 'FARHAN', 'SHRIDHAR', 'VAMIKA'],\n    'Age': [14, 25, 55, 8, 21]\n}, index=pd.date_range('2010-10-09 08:45', periods=5, freq='h'))\nprint(\"Original DataFrame:\")\nprint(df)\ntransposed_df = df.transpose()\nprint(\"\\nTransposed DataFrame:\")\nprint(transposed_df)\n\nOutput:Transposing Mixed Data TypesExample 4: Handling Missing Values in TransposeThe transpose() function also handles missing values in the same way as other operation.\n            Python\n\n    import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\"A\": [12, 4, 5, None, 1],\"B\": [7, 2, 54, 3, None],\"C\": [20, 16, 11, 3, 8],\"D\": [14, 3, None, 2, 6]},\n                  index=['Row_1', 'Row_2', 'Row_3', 'Row_4', 'Row_5'])\nprint(\"Original DataFrame:\")\nprint(df)\ntransposed_df = df.transpose()\nprint(\"\\nTransposed DataFrame:\")\nprint(transposed_df)\n\nOutput:Handling Missing ValuesTransposing a DataFrame is simple in Pandas library which  allows us to quickly change the way our data is structured and helps in exploring it from a different perspective.",
  "url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/pandas/python-pandas-dataframe-transpose"
}
